module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.module_outside_every_compiled_population_rots_silently

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data module_outside_every_compiled_population_rots_silently: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "module_outside_every_compiled_population_rots_silently" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a module that no required run compiles stops compiling, and main stays green** INVALID STATE: an accepted source file on main that the compiler refuses when asked to compile it -- a production module or a witness alike -- while every required lane reports SUCCESS, because no required lane's population contains it. The file is not red; it is outside every population that could turn red.",

    "WHY IT IS A CLASS AND NOT A SERIES OF SLIPS. Every upstream change that tightens a wall, changes a signature or moves a declaration is census-checked over exactly the population its own run compiles. The consumers inside that population are fixed in the same change, which is correct and is what makes the change look complete. The consumers outside it are stranded by the same motion, and nothing reports them, because the gate that would report them is the gate whose population they are outside. DESIGN section 3 names the rule (a root outside the gate: enumerate its consumers by name before you delete); this row is what happens when the change is not a deletion and nobody thinks the rule applies. The decay is monotone: each stranded file is stranded forever unless something compiles it, and every later edit stacks a second stranding on the first.",

    "HOW IT DIFFERS FROM ITS NEIGHBOURS. [[witness_outside_gate_closure_falsified_by_other_file]] is a witness that still COMPILES and whose ASSERTION a change elsewhere falsified; [[witness_that_fails_to_compile_is_absent_rather_than_red]] is a witness MERGED already uncompilable. This row is the enabling condition beneath both at population grain: any module, witness or production, that compiled when it landed and was made uncompilable later by a change to a DIFFERENT file, with no run anywhere that compiles it. Production modules are in scope and are the worse half -- extdeps.version.pep440, gunbc.repo.repo_ruleset and gunbc.auth.patterns were among the refused -- because nothing but their own compilation would ever read them.",

    "RECEIPT, 2026-09-28. quick-crab-850's measured floor for #12381 (run 36422057907) reached about 82 percent of the corpus and refused 576 errors in 33 files #12381 did not edit. Re-compiled one by one on origin/main 5bf2b13fb8 with the compile-clean argv (three roots, primary-precedence, --entry, --target dag --dry-run), 24 of the 34 files named (33 plus the effect-summary module) refused with 126 located blocking errors, and 10 compiled clean under their own closure. The 24 fell to a handful of upstream CAUSES, none of them the file's own edit: #11720 (refuse an Optional where a Required value is declared) stranded about 15 files whose .first() and lookup results reached T-typed parameters -- that wall's census fixed 94 in-population sites and could not see these; #11303 added the required history argument to roadmap_authority_projection; #11565 added container_env to endpoint_incarnation_observation; the SshShell transport became a record carrying ssh_host; and #12205 (refuse an unimported bare provider) stranded six modules that had named LiveTreeDisposition or fold_list without importing them. Three residues were not caller defects and are out of this row's repair: two witnesses refused by what reads as a checker defect in resolving a declared alias (List as FreeMonoid, a cross-module TokenThroughput alias), and gunbc.auth.patterns, whose rot runs past its imports to a callee defined nowhere. Every one of those upstream PRs was green and correct for the population it compiled.",

    "SECOND RECEIPT, AND IT SHARPENS THE CEILING. The 10 files that compiled clean per-entry carried floor errors that exist only in the floor's one prepared subject: ambiguous bare references (json_string, placement_verdict, render, settle) and unresolved types that resolve inside the file's own closure. So even a gate that compiled every file as its own entry would not reach the whole class: a module's verdict is a property of the CLOSURE it is compiled in. The gate that closes this row compiles the whole corpus as one subject, under the same scope construction the floor uses.",

    "HARM: a consumer that stays broken is not a latent risk but a present one. A production module outside the population cannot be built into anything the moment someone reaches for it, and the first person to do so pays for every stranding stacked on it since, in someone else's session and under someone else's brief -- which is how this receipt was found: #12381's floor had to refuse on 33 files it never touched. That is section 2's deferred cost moved onto whichever change next widens a population.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable, and only by accident -- the failure is contained because nothing executes the broken module, not because anything detects it. The declared drop [[required_gate_bankruptcy]] (Required gate reduced to the compiler floor, 2026-08-29) is the standing reason the population is narrow, and the RequiredFloorDisposition arm DeclinedOutsideGateClosure is where this population is counted as declined rather than compiled.",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: 2, mechanically preventable. Whether an accepted file compiles is decidable, so the class admits a wall; it cannot reach 3 or 4 because the invalid state is produced by an edit to a different file, which no construction in the stranded file forbids. The wall is a required gate that compiles the WHOLE corpus as one prepared subject on every change, or equivalently one whose population is the reverse-dependency closure of every changed declaration, run under the floor's own scope construction.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, naming the capability: a required lane whose compiled population is the whole corpus (or the transitive reverse-dependency closure of the change, provably a superset of every consumer of every changed declaration) under the floor's prepared-subject scope, whose refusal blocks the merge. The trigger is not met by a per-entry compile of changed files, which misses the closure-scoped half measured above, and not by any one-off full compile, which clears today's population and restores nothing: the population re-rots on the next change.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.required_floor", decl_name: "required_gate_prefixes", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.required_floor", decl_name: "RequiredFloorDisposition", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.rung_drop.required_gate_bankruptcy", decl_name: "required_gate_bankruptcy", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.witness_outside_gate_closure_falsified_by_other_file", decl_name: "witness_outside_gate_closure_falsified_by_other_file", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.witness_that_fails_to_compile_is_absent_rather_than_red", decl_name: "witness_that_fails_to_compile_is_absent_rather_than_red", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
